WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Still images preserve discrete moments with potentially high detail.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.040
Video captures movement and sequence but creates more data and may reduce per-frame

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:08.640
clarity.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.880
Choose the format that answers the objective rather than recording both by default.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.515
Altitude and angle shape perspective.

00:00:14.515 --> 00:00:17.552
Higher views cover more area but usually show less detail.

00:00:17.552 --> 00:00:19.800
Oblique views reveal building sides and terrain shape,

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.840
while near-vertical views support plan-style comparison.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.040
Angle also changes scale, occlusion, and apparent distance.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.046
Field of view is the area visible through the camera or sensor.

00:00:29.046 --> 00:00:32.436
Lens choice, altitude, orientation, and cropping affect coverage.

00:00:32.436 --> 00:00:35.930
Record enough context to locate the subject without collecting a wide area that the

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:37.395
mission does not need.

00:00:37.395 --> 00:00:41.535
Overlap helps mapping and before-and-after comparison by ensuring adjacent views share

00:00:41.535 --> 00:00:42.355
features.

00:00:42.355 --> 00:00:46.470
Consistent orientation, route, altitude, and capture interval improve repeatability.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.985
However, repeated collection should remain within the authorized purpose.

00:00:49.985 --> 00:00:50.995
Stable capture matters.

00:00:50.995 --> 00:00:53.570
Sudden motion, vibration, poor focus, haze, glare,

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.680
and aggressive camera movement can make footage unusable.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.370
Pause or repeat a safe capture rather than rushing through a checklist while conditions

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:01.115
deteriorate.

00:01:01.115 --> 00:01:03.815
Review a sample for exposure, focus, framing, storage,

00:01:03.815 --> 00:01:06.120
and metadata when operationally safe.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.145
Clear imagery does not remove uncertainty,

00:01:08.145 --> 00:01:12.115
but poor imagery can create false detail and unreliable interpretation.

00:01:12.115 --> 00:01:14.255
Quality begins with deliberate collection.

00:01:14.255 --> 00:01:16.830
Collection teams should prefer a small set of well-documented,

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.520
usable images over a large volume of rushed footage.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.710
Excess data increases review effort and can obscure the evidence that actually answers the

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:24.040
objective.
